#summary-header

#no-indent[
Cooling equipment for public space is sold on outlet ratings. A bus shelter lives at the bench: jets mix, dive, and flush before comfort arrives. We score *delivered* cooling with a three-layer chain. M1 asks whether cold air arrives, using exchange, short-circuit mixing $eta_"mix"$, humidity-aware pad efficiency, and an optional latent offset, so the seat fraction has a physical origin. M2 converts air state into felt cooling with a capped air-speed credit. M3 builds a composite delivery index (CEI) from felt benefit, specific cooling, seat-weighted safety coverage, energy cost, water, and occupancy coverage, then applies hard safety and lifecycle-budget gates. Rated power stays out of the index because it would only repeat what the energy term already captures.

An analytic humidity crossover remains the signature result. With fixed pad efficiency it sits near 64% RH at the Tianjin baseline; with $epsilon_p("RH")$ it moves to about *54%*, so humid cities favor fans earlier. Single-factor ablation shows that platform “flips” come from more than internal gain: gain taxes the fan path asymmetrically, while exchange raises evaporative retention. On a \$3000 open-shelter budget only the 0.25 kW fan is feasible (CEI $approx 0.60$); the larger evaporative unit shows a *lower* delivery fraction than the mid-size unit when short-circuit strength is higher. At 42 °C the feasible set can be empty even when dry-air physics looks strong. Choose by the humidity at the hour of use; treat shade, aiming, coverage, safety, and budget as one purchase.
]
